## Pitfalls

- **Line emission inside the continuum channels.** Leads to over-subtraction (negative bowls at line positions)
  and a biased continuum. 2016AJ....152..218I redetermined the continuum "using only edge data points to avoid
  over-subtraction"; 2013ApJ...774L...7M admits the continuum may be line-contaminated. In NGC 253, lines made up
  5.3% to 36.6% of the total flux per 50 GHz bin (2021A&A...656A..46M), so a line-rich "continuum" image can be
  badly contaminated.
- **Absorption plus uv-plane subtraction.** Default visibility-domain subtraction left negative residuals near CS
  in 2014ApJ...792L..25V (they subtracted an average artifact from line-free channels); 2015ApJ...814...22E moved to
  the image plane for this reason.
- **STATCONT near the noise.** Per-pixel STATCONT overestimated the continuum in noisy regions and was "not
  optimal" for lines near the noise level (2021A&A...656A..46M).
- **High fit order with one-sided channels.** A linear fit extrapolated across a broad line or from one side of the
  spectrum is unconstrained (general practice). Papers that had line-free channels only on one side said so
  (2017A&A...606A.125S, N2D+ channels 350-750); prefer order 0 in that case.
- **Few line-free channels.** Papers fell back to the other sideband or an SED for the continuum
  (2020ApJ...905...85S) or extrapolated the broad spw to narrow spws (2019A&A...624L...7V).
- **Uncertainties.** Formal line errors ignore continuum-subtraction systematics (2019ApJ...882..118X says its
  uncertainties "might be underestimated" for this reason). Add a term, as 2013ApJ...773L..34K did (10%).
- **Missing flux is separate.** Interferometric filtering is a different error that the subtraction does not fix
  (2013ApJ...775L..31S, 2014A&A...569A..11S); do not attribute negative bowls to subtraction without checking
  short-spacing coverage.
- **Reporting.** Most cards cannot reproduce the step: line-free ranges, fit order and uvcontsub settings are
  routinely missing (pack "not stated" list: line-free channels 33, line-free channels used for continuum
  subtraction 28, line-free channels for uvcontsub 16, line-free channels and uvcontsub fit order 7, among other
  variants). Good reporting examples list the channels per spw (2018MNRAS.481.4931E: SPW0 25-118; SPW1 50-75,
  90-118; SPW2 all; SPW3 9-35, 95-119) or the frequency ranges (2018ApJ...863..143C, 2017MNRAS.472.1239B).

## Verification checks

Checks papers ran:

- Image with and without subtraction and compare (2018A&A...609A..47P "no significant difference";
  2024ApJ...972..111L, the redshifted [C II] component persists without uvcontsub; 2020ApJ...889L..24P,
  2018ApJ...868..113T made both cubes).
- Compare uv-plane against image-plane subtraction (2019A&A...629A..75B, 2019ApJ...883L..29L) or against a
  second uv implementation (2017MNRAS.469.4683C: GILDAS UV_SUBTRACT check agrees to < 5%).
- Vary the fit order and channel ranges (2012ApJ...757..129R; 2019A&A...628A.118B, first order did not
  significantly change results; 2019Natur.570...83M, different line-free sets changed spectra by <= 7%).
- Try another continuum estimate: alternate sideband (2020ApJ...905...85S, "no appreciable differences"); the
  constant from a Gaussian-plus-constant fit should match the continuum measured from line-free spws
  (2013ApJ...770...13W, 120 uJy).
- Residual flatness: residual slope < 30 mJy across each spw (2019Natur.570...83M); the pack's validation
  aggregate also lists "outside-line signals in continuum-subtracted spectra became almost zero" and "residual
  inspected in emission-free channels".
- Noise calibration from line-free channels: scale errors so Var(S/sigma) = 1 in line-free channels
  (2019MNRAS.485.2092C).
- Compare total line flux against single-dish data where available (2014ApJ...797...90S, 76-97% recovered;
  2024MNRAS.527.9343L against JCMT).

What a careful referee adds:

- Ask for the exact line-free ranges per spw and the fit order, or the STATCONT settings.
- Look for negative bowls in moment-0 maps at line positions and for line residue in the continuum image at line
  peaks.
- For line-rich sources, compare the continuum image against one made from a strict, small channel set.
- For absorption studies, confirm the continuum level used for optical depth came from data that were not
  continuum-subtracted.

